the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124503 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28dfc208-68c2-5f88-8bd4-209d53beb5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282042} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab5ec49-12b7-5d47-bbb5-fdb3e615e28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282327} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac90f37-c08a-5239-9525-835adc2e3f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}<3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282456} {:answer/id #uuid "90a39220-b826-5895-8d22-71811c5c78a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282940} {:answer/id #uuid "d51e3388-cf35-5dcb-8bc1-829f1f429a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ab5ec49-12b7-5d47-bbb5-fdb3e615e28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282327} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac34746-de0d-5226-892a-e787c1d531e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc95e142-b514-5431-9924-bdc1db9331dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-164307" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =(z-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-3)^{2} & =(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\ z & =3\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the circle $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124555 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38fc56c-0b29-550e-b109-d771f89562da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391953} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c9da7-de70-5aea-8bf7-5c9b4d287b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} {:answer/id #uuid "003b79b6-3c82-5aed-b7a9-37fc9aef1484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391955} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa71829-34e0-581f-aa98-5fcdef3852dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $z=2x\\pm 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391956} {:answer/id #uuid "f376e4eb-51f9-5938-9cba-d55cb6cf1e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse: $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c9da7-de70-5aea-8bf7-5c9b4d287b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391954} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d57934b-d5eb-5a5a-8448-b8cae29a9df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcfedd1b-1d6b-51cf-8ffc-3860006da14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36288" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $y=2$, we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$"}]}
